\title{Kernels and scales of effect}
\description{
\code{multiScaleR} summarizes the landscape around each survey point with a
\emph{kernel}, a function that gives nearby raster cells more weight than distant
ones. The width of the kernel is its scale parameter, sigma, measured in map
units. This page defines the four kernel families, explains which covariates
use smooth kernel weights and which use a hard radius, shows how sigma is
converted into a distance you can report, and explains how to choose
\code{max_D}. For worked examples, see the section "Understand the kernel and its
parameters" in \code{vignette("v02-user-guide", package = "multiScaleR")}.
}
\section{What a kernel does}{
The \emph{scale of effect} is the distance over which a landscape variable
influences a response measured at a survey point. Instead of fitting one
model per buffer radius, \code{multiScaleR} weights every raster cell around a
point by its distance and estimates the kernel width from your data, along
with the regression coefficients.

For a survey point and a raster layer, \code{\link[=kernel_prep]{kernel_prep()}} extracts the cells
within \code{max_D} map units of the point and records the distance \code{d} from the
point to each cell center. A kernel-weighted covariate is then the weighted
mean of those cell values \code{v}:

\deqn{x = \frac{\sum_i w(d_i) v_i}{\sum_i w(d_i)}}{x = sum(w(d) * v) / sum(w(d))}

The sums run over cells with a finite value. A missing cell drops out of
both sums, so the remaining weights renormalize. Because the weight
function appears above and below the line, any normalizing constant
cancels and only the shape of \code{w(d)} matters. The model then uses this
covariate after centering and scaling, as described in \code{\link[=kernel_prep]{kernel_prep()}}.

Sigma is estimated by \code{\link[=multiScale_optim]{multiScale_optim()}} (or \code{\link[=multiScale_bayes]{multiScale_bayes()}}). It is
always in the linear units of the projection, usually meters.
}

\section{Choose a kernel family}{
Set the kernel with the \code{kernel} argument of \code{\link[=kernel_prep]{kernel_prep()}}. All covariates
prepared together share one kernel, so comparing kernels means one
\code{\link[=kernel_prep]{kernel_prep()}} call per kernel. The weight functions below come from the
package code.\tabular{llll}{
   Kernel \tab \verb{kernel =} \tab Weight of a cell at distance \code{d} \tab Estimated parameters \cr
   Gaussian (default) \tab \code{"gaussian"} \tab \code{exp(-d^2 / (2 * sigma^2))} \tab sigma \cr
   Negative exponential \tab \code{"exp"} \tab \code{exp(-d / sigma)} \tab sigma \cr
   Exponential power \tab \code{"expow"} \tab \code{exp(-(d / sigma)^shape)} \tab sigma and shape \cr
   Fixed buffer \tab \code{"fixed"} \tab 1 when \code{d < sigma}, 0 otherwise \tab sigma, which is the buffer radius \cr
}


How to read the parameters:
\itemize{
\item \strong{Gaussian.} Sigma is the standard deviation of a normal curve. A cell
at distance sigma gets about 61\% of the weight of a cell at the point,
and a cell at twice sigma gets about 14\%.
\item \strong{Negative exponential.} Sigma is the decay length. A cell at distance
sigma gets about 37\% (1/e) of the weight at the point. The weight drops
faster near the point than under a Gaussian kernel and has a longer tail.
\item \strong{Exponential power.} A cell at distance sigma always gets about 37\% of
the weight at the point, and \code{shape} sets the form of the curve. With
\code{shape = 1} the kernel is the negative exponential with the same sigma.
With \code{shape = 2} it has the Gaussian form, with a standard deviation of
sigma / sqrt(2). Larger values flatten the top and steepen the edge, so the
kernel approaches a fixed buffer of radius sigma. Values below 1 give a
sharper peak and a heavier tail. \code{\link[=kernel_prep]{kernel_prep()}} starts the shape at 2,
and the default refit search of \code{\link[=multiScale_optim]{multiScale_optim()}} keeps it between
0.75 and 50.
\item \strong{Fixed buffer.} Every cell whose center lies within sigma gets equal
weight, so the covariate is the plain mean of those cells.
}

Start with the Gaussian kernel. Fit the other kernels when you have a reason
to test a different decay shape, and compare the fits by AICc with
\code{\link[=aic_tab]{aic_tab()}}. Not every fitting route accepts every kernel, as
\link{multiScaleR-fitting-methods} explains.
}

\section{Smooth kernels and hard radii}{
A covariate's scale is either a smooth kernel width or a hard radius, and
the two behave differently during estimation.

\strong{Smooth kernel weights.} Kernel means from \code{\link[=kernel_var]{kernel_var()}} and
kernel-weighted surface metrics from \code{surface_var(..., weighted = TRUE)} use
the Gaussian, negative exponential, or exponential power weights above.
Each weight changes continuously with sigma, so the likelihood is a smooth
function of sigma. \code{\link[=multiScale_optim]{multiScale_optim()}} can then report a Hessian-based
(Wald) standard error and confidence interval for sigma.

\strong{Hard radius.} Some covariates count each cell fully or not at all:
\itemize{
\item Every landscape metric from \code{\link[=landscape_var]{landscape_var()}}.
\item Every surface metric from \code{\link[=surface_var]{surface_var()}} with \code{weighted = FALSE} (the
default).
\item Every optimized covariate prepared with \code{kernel = "fixed"}, including
kernel means and weighted surface metrics.
}

The estimated scale of such a covariate is a radius in map units. The
covariate changes only when the radius passes the distance of a cell
center, so the likelihood is a step function of the radius. It is flat
between steps and jumps at them, which leaves no useful curvature for a
Hessian standard error. The default summary therefore shows \code{NA} for the
standard error and Wald interval of a hard radius. Examine the likelihood
profile instead, with \code{\link[=profile_sigma]{profile_sigma()}} or \code{summary(fit, profile = TRUE)},
and report the estimate as a radius in map units. An interval read from a
profile cutoff does not by itself establish coverage for a stepped
likelihood. Because a flat objective can stall a search at its start,
\code{\link[=multiScale_optim]{multiScale_optim()}} screens several starting values by default when the
kernel is \code{"fixed"}.

Under \code{kernel = "expow"}, a hard-radius covariate has no shape parameter.
Its reported shape and shape standard error are \code{NA}, and model selection
leaves that unused position out of the parameter count.

A metric given a fixed \code{radius} in \code{\link[=landscape_var]{landscape_var()}} or \code{\link[=surface_var]{surface_var()}} has
no scale to estimate. It is computed once and enters the model as an
ordinary covariate.
}

\section{Report an effective distance}{
Sigma is a parameter, not a radius. The same sigma reaches a different
distance under each kernel, so report an \emph{effective distance}, the distance
that holds a stated share \code{prob} of the kernel weight (90\% by default).
\code{\link[=kernel_dist]{kernel_dist()}} makes the conversion, \code{\link[=plot_kernel]{plot_kernel()}} draws it, and
\code{summary()} of a fitted model reports it next to each sigma.

Two definitions are available through the \code{dimension} argument:
\itemize{
\item \strong{Planar, \code{dimension = "2d"} (the default since version 0.7.4).} Raster
cells lie on a plane, and the number of cells at distance \code{d} grows with
the circumference 2 pi \code{d}. The planar distance is the radius of the
circle around the point that holds \code{prob} of the total kernel weight.
Use it when you describe the area a kernel draws on.
\item \strong{Line-based, \code{dimension = "1d"}.} The kernel is treated as a curve along
a single transect through the point. The distance encloses \code{prob} of the
area under that curve. This was the default before version 0.7.4.
}\tabular{llll}{
   Kernel \tab Line-based distance (\code{"1d"}) \tab Planar radius (\code{"2d"}) \tab At \code{prob = 0.9}, 1D and 2D \cr
   Gaussian \tab \code{qnorm((1 + prob) / 2) * sigma} \tab \code{sigma * sqrt(-2 * log(1 - prob))} \tab 1.645 and 2.146 times sigma \cr
   Negative exponential \tab \code{-sigma * log(1 - prob)} \tab \code{sigma * qgamma(prob, shape = 2)} \tab 2.303 and 3.890 times sigma \cr
   Exponential power \tab \code{sigma * qgamma(prob, shape = 1 / shape)^(1 / shape)} \tab \code{sigma * qgamma(prob, shape = 2 / shape)^(1 / shape)} \tab Depends on \code{shape} \cr
   Fixed buffer \tab \code{sigma * prob} \tab \code{sigma * sqrt(prob)} \tab 0.900 and 0.949 times sigma \cr
}


For example, a Gaussian kernel with sigma = 300 m holds 90\% of its planar
weight within about 644 m, while the line-based 90\% distance is about
493 m. On an unbounded plane, the circle of radius 493 m holds only about
74\% of the weight. A hard-radius covariate reports its radius under either
definition. Always state the definition and the share of weight you report.

Both definitions describe the ideal, continuous kernel on an unbounded
plane. They ignore truncation at \code{max_D}, raster edges, missing cells, and
cell geometry, so the share of weight held by the cells actually extracted
around a point can differ. The \code{pct_wt} smoothing window of
\code{\link[=kernel_scale.raster]{kernel_scale.raster()}}, the default \code{max_D} of \code{\link[=sim_dat]{sim_dat()}}, and the
\code{max_D} warning of \code{\link[=multiScale_optim]{multiScale_optim()}} keep the line-based definition.
}

\section{Choose max_D}{
\code{max_D}, set in \code{\link[=kernel_prep]{kernel_prep()}}, does three jobs.
\itemize{
\item \strong{It sets the neighborhood.} Each point gets every raster cell that
intersects a circle of radius \code{max_D} around it, including partly covered
cells at the edge, which keep their full kernel weight. Cells beyond
\code{max_D} are never seen, so each kernel is cut off at \code{max_D} and its
weights renormalize over the extracted cells.
\item \strong{It bounds the search.} \code{\link[=multiScale_optim]{multiScale_optim()}} estimates sigma, or a hard
radius, between \code{min_D} (the spacing of adjacent cell centers, about one
cell width) and \code{max_D}. \code{\link[=multiScale_bayes]{multiScale_bayes()}} places priors on sigma
without these hard bounds, but its covariates still use only the cells
extracted within \code{max_D}.
\item \strong{It sets the cost.} A larger \code{max_D} extracts more cells per point,
which costs preparation time and memory. With \code{bin = TRUE} (the default)
the cost of optimizing kernel means no longer depends on \code{max_D}. The
raster must cover each point's full buffer.
}

Set \code{max_D} above the largest scale you consider plausible. After fitting,
\code{\link[=multiScale_optim]{multiScale_optim()}} flags an estimate whose line-based 90\% distance is more
than half of \code{max_D}. It prints a warning (when \code{verbose = TRUE}), records
the \code{max_distance} entry of \code{\link[=diagnostics]{diagnostics()}}, and suggests a larger \code{max_D}
in its \code{next_run} element. For a Gaussian kernel the flag appears once sigma
exceeds about \code{max_D / 3.3}, and for a negative exponential kernel once it
exceeds about \code{max_D / 4.6}. So for a plausible upper sigma \code{s}, choose \code{max_D} of at
least 3.3 \code{s} (Gaussian) or 4.6 \code{s} (negative exponential). Also compare
the planar radius from \code{kernel_dist(sigma = s, kernel = ...)} with \code{max_D}.
When that radius approaches \code{max_D}, the extracted cells hold noticeably
less of the kernel weight than the ideal kernel describes. If an estimate
comes back near \code{max_D}, increase \code{max_D}, run \code{\link[=kernel_prep]{kernel_prep()}} again, and
refit. Do not shrink a scientifically chosen \code{max_D} only to save memory.
\code{\link[=estimate_multiscale_ram]{estimate_multiscale_ram()}} describes leaner preparation options.
}

